  "query_preview": "..\n(This afternoon through next Thursday)\n\nWEATHER PATTERN OVERVIEW: Calm weather conditions expected to be\nthe dominant factor today and tomorrow with unseasonably warm\ntemperatures as highs will be several degrees above normal to near\n90 degrees. This evening and overnight, slight chances (10-30%) of\nsome rain showers over northern and central Nevada as tropical\nmoisture from the remnants of the Pacific Hurricanes move across\nthe state today. Trace amounts of precipitation may reach into the\nvalleys overnight as temperatures cool, but much of the rain is\nexpected over mountain peaks. Weather clears out Friday to sunny\nconditions and above normal temperatures, with winds increasing in\nthe afternoon up to 10-15 mph, gusts 20-25 mph as a low pressure\nsystem to the northwest begins to push into northwestern CONUS,\ntightening weather gradients. By Saturday, strong gusty winds\nexpected as the gradients tighten further with wind speeds up to\n20-25 mph, gusts up to 35-45 mph. High temperatures will still be\na few degrees above normal, but cooler air begins to move in\nkeeping highs in the 80s with much of the cold air reaching by\nmidnight as lows drop into the 40s with a few valleys into the 30s.\nSunday, winds will be much weaker with a few breezy gusts up to \n20 mph in the afternoons. Temperatures shift to the opposite\nspectrum with highs now expected to be several degrees below\nnormal this time of year with highs in the 60s-70s (80s for parts\nof central Nevada in N. Nye County and White Pine County).\nOvernight lows drop to near freezing temperatures in the 30s and\n40s with some valleys below freezing. Monday is expected to see\nmuch the same as Sunday with highs several degrees below normal\nand overnight lows in the 30s to 40s. By Tuesday and into mid-\nweek, temperatures will slightly warm up but stay within normal.",
